WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.260 --> 00:00:08.780
Rob Jenkins' life was turned upside down in 2008 when he was convicted of growing marijuana in his apartment.

00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:14.180
I don't know who told on me or what but police ran in there, took my whole garden apart,

00:00:14.180 --> 00:00:16.980
trashed my whole apartment.

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:21.540
I got evicted from the apartment. I got a notice like two weeks later that said get the hell out.

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:27.620
Jenkins managed to avoid jail time with a plea deal but his criminal record made it difficult to find a job,

00:00:27.620 --> 00:00:30.960
so he went back to illegally growing and dealing marijuana.

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:38.660
I had to sell clones and pretty much risk going back to jail, while on probation. For three years.

00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:41.860
So it was pretty much just risking my life.

00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:46.100
Jenkins is one of an estimated 20 million people nationwide, with a criminal record

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:48.960
related to marijuana use or distribution,

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:53.020
living in a state where recreational cannabis is now legal.

00:00:53.020 --> 00:00:56.380
In a move that's reminiscent of the final years of the Prohibition era,

00:00:56.380 --> 00:01:01.980
when then President Franklin Roosevelt issued some 1,300 pardons for alcohol-related offenses,

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:06.180
officials are now looking for ways to clear these convictions from the public record.

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:10.760
With a new year come new laws that take effect today in California and among them,

00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:12.860
the legalization of marijuana.

00:01:12.860 --> 00:01:15.280
It's going to send a beacon of hope

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:22.600
out to all of the other states, and to all of the other countries across the world where cannabis is still illegal.

00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:26.140
When California voters legalized cannabis in 2016,

00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:30.880
they also approved a proposition that allowed the state to expunge past convictions.

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:37.140
But the law hasn't worked as intended. There are too many bureaucratic hurdles in the path to expungement.

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:43.380
But the way the legislation was written really kind of put it all on the people that have been convicted, and,

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:47.760
it didn't prohibit us from doing anything about it didn't also spell out that you should.

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:51.660
San Francisco district attorney George Gascon became aware of this problem

00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:57.400
when his office first started tackling the process of identifying records eligible for removal from the system.

00:01:57.400 --> 00:02:02.380
In 2017 the city had over 9,000 residents eligible for expungement

00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:06.300
but only 23 people had petitioned to wipe their records clean.

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:12.440
We know that statewide the numbers of people seeking relief for this type of stuff is usually around 5 or 10%.

00:02:12.440 --> 00:02:17.520
So there's a really small number and frankly often the people that seek relief, well they're more sophisticated.

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:20.080
They understand the law is there, they can hire a lawyer,

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:25.440
or even if they don't have the money hire a lawyer, they go proactively to a expungement clinic.

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:29.096
They do this on their own. Grascon turned to Silicon Valley for help.

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:33.340
Partnering with a non profit, Code for America, to come up with a technological solution

00:02:33.340 --> 00:02:35.240
to the problem of too much red tape.

00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:41.660
A criminal record is an enormous barrier to jobs, to stable housing, to education

00:02:41.660 --> 00:02:44.820
to being able to engage in your kids school activities.

00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:50.420
Ivonne Silva is Code for America's senior program director. How do we reduce those barriers?

00:02:50.420 --> 00:02:57.000
And we started to build an early technology that would help an individual go through that process on their own.

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:02.020
In building that technology we ask the question well why do people have to apply at all?

00:03:02.020 --> 00:03:08.220
Why petition at all when we know that the government has the information to evaluate eligibility,

00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:10.520
to provide this relief automatically?

00:03:10.520 --> 00:03:14.920
Silva's group is aiming to clear a quarter million cannabis-related convictions in California

00:03:14.920 --> 00:03:16.560
by the end of the year.

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:20.420
It's using an algorithm to flag records eligible for expungement,

00:03:20.420 --> 00:03:25.180
then software automatically files the necessary paperwork to clear the conviction.

00:03:25.180 --> 00:03:29.220
While San Francisco was the first county in  the state to implement this new program,

00:03:29.220 --> 00:03:34.240
Code for America is partnering with other jurisdictions, including Los Angeles and San Jose,

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:39.440
to launch a pilot program it's hoping eventually to apply its technology nationwide.

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:43.240
The technologys actually very simple, but it also starts to shift

00:03:43.240 --> 00:03:48.920
the way in which people relate to their government because now this is a service provided to government,

00:03:48.920 --> 00:03:52.280
as opposed to government, being seen as an obstacle.

00:03:52.280 --> 00:03:58.520
Jenkins, who now works in the legal cannabis industry and is hoping to start his own medical grow operation

00:03:58.520 --> 00:04:02.740
paid thousands of dollars in attorney fees to get his conviction expunged.

00:04:02.740 --> 00:04:07.976
I feel kind of validated you know, I know people are still in jail for it you know so that so it's legal,

00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:13.480
but there's still people in jail right now, for cannabis related offenses. I know eventually

00:04:13.480 --> 00:04:15.100
they might get released and eventually,

00:04:15.100 --> 00:04:19.640
you know they might have their record expunged but I mean it's days lost, time lost,

00:04:19.640 --> 00:04:24.780
I lost everything when I got arrested. That was a lot of resources that I could have used towards the business

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:26.980
I'm trying to start from scratch right now.

